This version counts one node and lets the helper count the rest of the list. When the pointer reaches `null`, the recursion returns `0`, which also handles an empty list.

For example, with `1 -> 2 -> 3`, the returned values add up as `1 + 1 + 1 + 0`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int getLength(ListNode head) {
        if (head == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        return 1 + getLength(head.next);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` because the recursive calls stay on the call stack. Very long lists can make recursion less safe than a loop.

A direct walk is enough here. Start at `head`, count the current node, and move to `next` until the pointer becomes `nullptr`.

The list itself is not changed, and an empty list correctly gives `0`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int getLength(ListNode* head) {
        int length = 0;
        ListNode* current = head;

        while (current != nullptr) {
            ++length;
            current = current->next;
        }

        return length;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because every node is visited once.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

This uses an explicit stack rather than counting while following the links. Every node is pushed first, then popped once, and the number of pops gives the length.

It is more space than needed for a linked list, but the two separate steps make the counting part easy to see. The input links stay unchanged.

```cpp
#include <stack>

class Solution {
public:
    int getLength(ListNode* head) {
        std::stack<ListNode*> pending;
        ListNode* current = head;

        while (current != nullptr) {
            pending.push(current);
            current = current->next;
        }

        int length = 0;
        while (!pending.empty()) {
            pending.pop();
            ++length;
        }

        return length;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` for pushing and popping all nodes.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stack. An empty list leaves the stack empty and returns `0`.

Instead of increasing the counter one node at a time, this loop safely checks two linked nodes together. Each full loop adds `2`, and one final node is added if the list has odd length.

For `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5`, the counter goes `0`, `2`, `4`, then the last node changes it to `5`. The checks before `current.next.next` prevent a null access.

```java
class Solution {
    public int getLength(ListNode head) {
        int length = 0;
        ListNode current = head;

        while (current != null && current.next != null) {
            length += 2;
            current = current.next.next;
        }

        if (current != null) {
            ++length;
        }

        return length;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since the whole list is still visited.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The empty list and both even and odd lengths are covered.

## Intuition
The key insight here is that the length of a linked list can be determined by traversing it once from head to tail. Each node contributes exactly one to the count, and since linked lists don’t allow random access, the only way to measure their size is sequential traversal. The invariant is: every step moves to the next node until nullptr is reached.

## Approach
Start at the head of the list.

Initialize a counter to 0.

Traverse node by node using a temporary pointer.

For each node visited, increment the counter.

Stop when the pointer reaches nullptr (end of list).

Return the counter as the length.

✅ Edge cases handled:

Empty list (head == nullptr) → counter remains 0.

Single node list → loop runs once, returns 1.

General case → correctly counts all nodes.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
public:
    int getLength(ListNode* head) {
        int count = 0;
        ListNode* temp = head;
        while(temp != nullptr){
            count++;
            temp = temp->next;
        }
        return count;
    }
};
```

